% =========================================
% ABSTRACT
% =========================================
\begin{abstract}
Client-side Adaptive Bitrate (ABR) control must jointly improve perceptual quality and avoid playback stalls, yet purely reward-maximizing deep reinforcement learning (DRL) often learns aggressive bitrate choices that inflate tail stall risk under throughput volatility.
This paper revisits DASH/HLS bitrate selection under a constrained Markov Decision Process (CMDP) viewpoint: maximize expected visual quality subject to empirical budgets on rebuffer accumulation and quality switching (\emph{smoothness}), using a primal--dual Lagrangian relaxation trained with Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO).
We augment training with informative client-side lookahead (near-future chunk information) and stabilize exploration using Lyapunov-style buffer-regularization signals present in our simulator reward.
Critically for deployment narratives, we also study deterministic \textit{runtime shielding}: a lightweight action projection layered on top of the learned policy to reduce perilous bitrate choices when playback risk is elevated. We instantiate both a full-time shield and a risk-gated variant that intervenes less frequently when risk is low, and optionally add mild shield-aware reward shaping plus hysteresis to encourage agreement with guard behavior.
Across a standardized trace-driven evaluation suite ($\VNepisodes$ paired episodes per method $\times$ four test videos under a policy-mode harness), constrained training achieves mean QoE \VQoNoShield\, in line with Pensieve (\VQoPen) / RobustMPC (\VQoMPC), whereas runtime shields cut mean session rebuffering from \VRbNoShield\,\%{} to roughly \VRbShieldLo\,--\,\VRbShieldHi\,\%{} across guarded configurations with QoE spanning \VQoShield\,--\,\VQoRiskGate.
Oracle Genie retains a gap (\VQoGenie\, QoE under identical logging) attributable to perfect trace knowledge rather than feasible client deployments.
Together, CMDP+Lagrangian training plus explicit runtime shielding offers a reproducible,\linebreak client-deployable abstraction for stall-aware streaming aligned with QoE-vs-safety evaluation.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Adaptive Bitrate Streaming, Deep Reinforcement Learning, Constrained MDP, Lagrangian Duality, Runtime Shielding, Video QoE.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

% =========================================
\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

\IEEEPARstart{H}{TTP}-based adaptive streaming is the default delivery mode for Internet video: clients fetch time-indexed segments from ordinary web caches and CDNs, while a local \emph{adaptive bitrate} (ABR) controller picks a representation (bitrate/resolution) for each short playback interval under time-varying throughput~\cite{sodagar2011mpeg,bentaleb2018survey}.
Because stall events are acutely salient to viewers and quality swings are distracting, modern systems must optimize a \emph{vector} of competing objectives---throughput efficiency, visual fidelity, rebuffering, and stability---rather than myopically maximizing a single scalar signal~\cite{wu2024qoe}.
Industry-scale demand for mobile and fixed broadband video further raises the stakes for robust client-side control when links are volatile or capacity-constrained~\cite{cisco2023}.

Operationally, the ABR policy observes partial state (buffer occupancy, recent throughput, manifest metadata) and issues a discrete action each \emph{chunk} period; download time couples stochastically to unknown future throughput, so the plant is inherently non-stationary.
Surveys consistently cluster solutions into buffer/threshold heuristics (e.g., BBA~\cite{huang2014buffer}), model-predictive style controllers (RobustMPC~\cite{yin2015control}), and learning-based policies (Pensieve~\cite{mao2017neural} and follow-ups) that replace hand-tuned rules with function approximation~\cite{bentaleb2018survey,wu2024qoe}.
Perceptual reporting increasingly relies on objective scores such as VMAF~\cite{li2016vmaf}, while evaluation still commonly linearizes trade-offs into a QoE surrogate that penalizes rebuffering and quality variation---a reporting convention we align with for comparability to widely cited MPC/DRL baselines.

Despite strong average-case performance, purely reward-maximizing deep RL can overfit simulators and learn behavior that is aggressive in the \emph{tail}: brief stalls under stress or sharp oscillations that are under-weighted in expectation but harmful in practice~\cite{sutton2018reinforcement,mao2017neural}.
Classical MPC mitigates some pathologies by explicit lookahead, yet its effectiveness depends on throughput predictors that may fail under distribution shift or multi-second volatility~\cite{yin2015control,sun2016cs2p}.
From a \emph{safe decision-making} perspective, this gap naturally suggests \emph{constrained} objectives: maintain high expected quality while keeping distributional proxies of stall and smoothness below operator-chosen budgets---a storyline aligned with constrained Markov Decision Processes (CMDPs)~\cite{altman1999constrained} and their Lagrangian / primal--dual surrogates studied in RL theory~\cite{paternain2019dual}.

\textbf{Technical positioning.}
We \emph{do not} claim formal worst-case safety certificates for neural policies in this paper; instead we pursue an engineering-realistic combination that is reproducible under a standardized simulator harness:
\textbf{(i)~Lagrangian CMDP-style training}, where dual variables adaptively reweight stall and smoothing penalties estimated from rollout statistics (projected ascent), instantiated with Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO)~\cite{schulman2017proximal} and closely related constrained policy learning ideas~\cite{achiam2017constrained};
\textbf{(ii)~future-aware observations} exploiting manifest-available near-future segment features (sizes, complexity indicators such as SI/TI)~\cite{sodagar2011mpeg}; and
\textbf{(iii)~deterministic runtime shielding}, i.e., a lightweight projection layer mapping proposed actions into a feasible/conservative subset before execution, analogous in spirit to logical shielding for RL~\cite{tian2022towards,xiao2025qoe,alshiekh2018shielding}.
This split mirrors a practical deployment pattern: approximate constraint satisfaction during training, then add an analyzable inference-time guard whose intervention rate can be measured and audited.

\textbf{Lyapunov-style cues (scope).}
To temper exploration when buffers are dangerously low or throughput estimates are pessimistic, we optionally consume simulator-exposed auxiliary signals resembling drift-aware regularizers~\cite{neely2010stochastic}.
We treat these signals as heuristic stabilizers (not substitutes for rigorous queue stability guarantees under neural approximation), which we state explicitly because reviewers often probe the boundary between motivational queueing intuition and RL claims~\cite{neely2010stochastic}.

\textbf{Contributions.} Relative to MPC baselines without learned predictors~\cite{yin2015control}, classical DRL ABR~\cite{mao2017neural,gadaleta2017ddash}, and QoE-centric safe RL studies~\cite{xiao2025qoe,tian2022towards}, this paper contributes:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{End-to-end CMDP+Lagrangian instantiation for adaptive streaming}: we cast session control as maximizing expected fidelity subject to smoothed surrogates of mean stall and bitrate switching, solved via primal--dual-style dual updates coherent with constrained RL duality~\cite{paternain2019dual}.

  \item \textbf{A future-aware Pensieve-class backbone with reproducible shields}: convolutional throughput history fused with lookahead/buffer features, complemented by deterministic runtime shields (\emph{classic} projection and \emph{risk-gated} intervention). Optional shield-aware shaping and bitrate hysteresis study train/inference coupling without asserting formal shield correctness proofs~\cite{alshiekh2018shielding}.

  \item \textbf{Evidence that foregrounds QoE vs.\ stall/switch trade-offs}: paired trace-driven episodes, bootstrap confidence intervals on episode means, headline nonparametric pairing, QoE/Rebuffer/visualization tooling, and disciplined reporting separating oracle Genie from deployable controllers~\cite{mao2017neural}.

\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Road map.}
Section~\ref{sec:related} situates heuristic, MPC, and learning-based ABR and connects to constrained/safe RL; Section~\ref{sec:system} formalizes QoE surrogates and CMDP constraints; Section~\ref{sec:method} details Lagrangian PPO training, shields, and algorithmic workflow; Section~\ref{sec:eval} answers RQs with aggregate tables, statistical tests, and figures; Sections~\ref{sec:limits}--\ref{sec:conclusion} discuss threats and summarize.

% =========================================
\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

We organize prior art along axes that mirror our design choices: classical QoE-centric control, MPC-style lookahead, purely learned bitrate policies, constrained/safe RL, and orthogonal system-level streaming research (edge-assisted delivery, QoE proxies). Table~\ref{tab:comparison} then condenses representative paradigms and positions our instantiation.

\subsection{HTTP adaptive streaming \& QoE-oriented control}

MPEG-DASH and similar standards decouple packaging from transport: the client obtains a ladder of representations plus chunk timing and nominally runs a bitrate policy purely from manifest-accessible cues and observable downloads~\cite{sodagar2011mpeg}.
Surveys document a large design space spanning buffer rules, predictor-assisted MPC, and learning~\cite{bentaleb2018survey,wu2024qoe}.
QoE surrogates typically trade average perceptual fidelity (bitrate or VMAF~\cite{li2016vmaf}) against stalls and instability; this multi-objective viewpoint is foundational for both classical and learning-based controllers~\cite{wu2024qoe}.

\textbf{Heuristic \& optimization-flavored controllers.}
Buffer-based policies such as BBA~\cite{huang2014buffer} demonstrate that simple occupancy rules can be surprisingly effective at scale, but they do not explicitly encode fine-grained horizon optimization.
BOLA reformulates ABR as an online optimization problem with provable structure under a Lyapunov-style stability argument for buffer evolution~\cite{spiteri2018bola}: this is spiritually related to drift-based safe RL~\cite{chow2018lyapunov} but instantiated with model-specific assumptions distinct from neural CMDP+PPO wrappers.
\textbf{Throughput prediction \& parameter adaptation.}
Throughput predictors underpin many MPC and hybrid designs; CS2P~\cite{sun2016cs2p} highlights data-driven forecasting as first-class coupling to bitrate selection quality.
\textbf{Systems-level orthogonal threads.}
Systems work on edge-assisted delivery~\cite{mehrabi2019edge,kim2023http,kang2023adaptive} and parameter auto-tuning (Oboe~\cite{akhtar2018oboe}) addresses \emph{where} bitrate decisions execute or \emph{how} knobs are calibrated, complementary to declaring explicit stall/smooth budgets on a neural policy itself.

\subsection{Model-predictive ABR \& lookahead rationale}

RobustMPC~\cite{yin2015control} embodies the dominant MPC narrative in ABR: solve a constrained short-horizon program under an estimated throughput trajectory.
When predictors track reality, MPC yields interpretable trade-offs; when they do not, conservative or aggressive mis-selection can appear---especially under bursty mobile links~\cite{kvalbein2014,sun2016cs2p}.
\textbf{Hybrid control--learning.}
Recent hybrid pipelines combine differentiable or structured predictors with learning~\cite{liu2023hybrid}; our framing differs by centering primal--dual constraint handling on rollout statistics~\cite{paternain2019dual} while retaining manifest lookahead features similar in spirit (though not identical in mechanism) to MPC horizon inputs~\cite{yin2015control}.

\subsection{Deep reinforcement learning for ABR}

Pensieve~\cite{mao2017neural} popularized RL on trace-driven emulation with recurrent throughput history and QoE-motivated rewards, catalyzing a family of successors (e.g., D-DASH contemporaneous deep RL formulations~\cite{gadaleta2017ddash}).
\textbf{Strengths.}
RL naturally consumes high-dimensional histories and heterogeneous manifest metadata without brittle hand-engineered surrogates for every impairment~\cite{sutton2018reinforcement}.
\textbf{Tensions for multimedia reviewers.}
Pure scalar rewards make stall and smoothness \emph{implicit}; multi-objective trade-offs then depend on reward scaling and simulator misspecification.
Field evidence (Fugu / ``Learning in Situ'') stresses deployment gaps between offline traces and live networks~\cite{yan2019learning}, motivating transparent reporting alongside average QoE.
These observations motivate declarative surrogates for stall/smooth budgets at training time~\cite{xiao2025qoe}---distinct from swapping in a heavier reward weight by hand~\cite{bentaleb2018survey}.

\subsection{Constrained, risk-aware, \& shielded RL}

CMDPs formulate expectations with auxiliary budget constraints~\cite{altman1999constrained}; primal--dual and Lagrangian relaxations underpin practical RL with constraint feedback~\cite{paternain2019dual}.
Policy-search analogues tighten trust regions under constraint surrogates~\cite{achiam2017constrained}.
\textbf{Safety narratives.}
Tutorial-style taxonomies categorize constraint satisfaction, pessimism, shields, Lyapunov methods, and more~\cite{tian2022towards}.
Lyapunov-style penalties appear as principled scaffolding in some safe RL recipes~\cite{chow2018lyapunov}; multimedia-specific treatments increasingly adopt CMDP viewpoints for QoE-sensitive streaming~\cite{xiao2025qoe}.

\textbf{Shields.}
Logical/runtime shielding prunes or repairs actions before execution to respect specifications~\cite{alshiekh2018shielding}.
For streaming, shields can be viewed as lightweight \emph{inference-time} stabilizers whose intervention frequency is measurable---a deployment-friendly complement to internal reward shaping when tail stall must be reduced without discarding the learned policy wholesale.

\subsection{Positioning of this work}

Relative to classical MPC~\cite{yin2015control}, we replace hand-rolled horizon optimization with a learned policy but retain explicit constraint signaling through Lagrangian duals~\cite{paternain2019dual} and manifest lookahead comparable to client-visible metadata~\cite{sodagar2011mpeg}.
Relative to Pensieve-style DRL~\cite{mao2017neural,gadaleta2017ddash}, we externalize stall/smoothness \emph{budgets} rather than burying them inside a single tuned scalar return.
Relative to QoE-aware safe RL works in streaming~\cite{xiao2025qoe}, we emphasize a reproducible split: dual updates during training plus deterministic shields at inference with logged intervention rates~\cite{alshiekh2018shielding}, aligning evaluation with both QoE and stall/switch summaries~\cite{wu2024qoe}.
We deliberately do \emph{not} blur these contributions with orthogonal systems optimizations (edge assistance~\cite{kang2023adaptive,kim2023http}, knob auto-tuning~\cite{akhtar2018oboe}) although such layers could stack atop our controllers in future deployments.

